{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1638 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a9404234753e8b1a68fc5b8d973338943a499d398788ea85084ed1812f7d912c" :edb/db-before-t 1637 :edb/db-after-t 1638 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534950 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:54:34.996Z" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124156 1100 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2$. Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$." 13194139534950 false] [17592186124156 1100 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,1,8)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y,$ find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124156 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2$. So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1)$.\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{f}_{y} ( 2 , 1 ) & = \\frac{4 ( 2 )}{( 1 )} - 4 ( 2 {)}^{2} ( 1 ) \\\\  & = 8 - 16 \\\\  & = - 8\\end{array}$$" 13194139534950 false] [17592186124156 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,1,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_y(2,1).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_y(x,y)$ at $(2,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =\\frac{4(2)}{(1)}-4(2)^{2}(1) \\\\  & =8-16 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124157 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z=f(x,y)$, with the plane $y=\\pi$. Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P$." 13194139534950 false] [17592186124157 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ Given that the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x = y\\cos(xy) + \\cos y,$ find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124157 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi$. So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg| _{(1,\\pi)}\n$$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}\n$$\n\nat $(1,\\pi)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\mid _{(1, \\pi )} &= \\pi \\cos (1 \\cdot \\pi ) + \\cos \\pi  \\\\\n&= \\pi (-1) + (-1) \\\\\n&= - \\pi  - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - z \\mid _{(1, \\pi )} &= \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\mid _{(1, \\pi )}(x - 1) \\\\\nz - (-1) &= (- \\pi  - 1)(x - 1) \\\\\nz + 1 &= (- \\pi  - 1)x + \\pi  + 1 \\\\\nz &= - (\\pi  + 1)x + \\pi \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi$.\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = \\pi \\\\ z = - ( \\pi  + 1 ) x + \\pi\\end{array} \\right.$$" 13194139534950 false] [17592186124157 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z = f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=\\pi.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x} \\Bigg|_{(1,\\pi)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\dfrac {\\partial z} {\\partial x}$ at $(1,\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi (-1)+(-1) \\\\  & =-\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,\\pi )} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,\\pi )}(x-1) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-\\pi -1)(x-1) \\\\ z+1 & =(-\\pi -1)x+\\pi +1 \\\\ z & =-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ has the equation $y=\\pi.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,\\pi,-1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\pi  \\\\ z=-(\\pi +1)x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124158 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z=f(x,y)$, with the plane $y=1$. Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)}$, find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$." 13194139534950 false] [17592186124158 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124158 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1$. So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1)$.\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{f}_{x} ( 0 , 1 ) & = \\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin   ( 2 ( 0 ) + \\frac{\\pi  ( 1 )}{4} ) \\\\  & = \\frac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) \\\\  & = \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz - f(0, 1) &= f_{x}(0, 1)(x - 0) \\\\\nz - 1 &= \\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\\nz &= \\frac{\\pi }{4}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1$.\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}y = 1 \\\\ z = \\frac{\\pi }{4} x + 1\\end{array} \\right.$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1$, we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle$.\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1$, we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle$.\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}{\\mathbf{n}}_{1} \\times  {\\mathbf{n}}_{2} & = \\langle  0 , 1 , 0 \\rangle  \\times  \\langle  - \\frac{\\pi }{4} , 0 , 1 \\rangle \\\\  & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ - \\pi  / 4 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\\\  & = \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\, \\mathbf{k} .\\end{array}$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{r}4 \\cdot  ( \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\, \\mathbf{k} ) = 4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\pi  \\, \\mathbf{k}\\end{array}$$" 13194139534950 false] [17592186124158 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0,1 \\right\\rangle.$\n\nComputing the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-\\frac{\\pi }{4},0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\pi /4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we've found a tangent vector, and this could be our final answer. However, just to make our answer a little nicer, we can multiply the vector by $4$ to yield a simpler, parallel vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot (i+\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,k)=4\\,i+\\pi \\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321952 13194139534950 true] [17592186124355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321959 13194139534950 true] [17592186124355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322007 13194139534950 true] [17592186124355 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322021 13194139534950 true] [17592186124356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321966 13194139534950 true] [17592186124356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321973 13194139534950 true] [17592186124356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321987 13194139534950 true] [17592186124356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322000 13194139534950 true] [17592186124356 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322028 13194139534950 true] [17592186124357 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321980 13194139534950 true] [17592186124357 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322014 13194139534950 true] [17592186124357 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322035 13194139534950 true] [17592186124357 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322042 13194139534950 true] [17592186124873 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534950 true] [17592186124873 1100 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z = f(x, y)$. The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y = 3$. Given that the point $P(2, 3, 9)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{x} = 6x - y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P$." 13194139534950 false] [17592186124873 1100 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=3.$ Given that the point $P(2,3,9)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6x-y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124873 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(2,3,9)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,3) & =6(2)-(3)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124893 1017 #edb/ref 17592186122380 13194139534950 false] [17592186124893 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321994 13194139534950 true] [17592186124893 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534950 true] [17592186124893 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S$, defined by $z = f(x, y)$, with the plane $y =-3$. Given that the point $P(2,-3, 3)$ lies on $C$, and that $f_{x} = 6(x - 1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$?" 13194139534950 false] [17592186124893 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(2,-3,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=6(x-1)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186124893 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(2,-3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(2,-3) & =6(2-1)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(2,-3) & ={f}_{x}(2,-3)(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6(x-2) \\\\ z-3 & =6x-12 \\\\ z & =6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-3 \\\\ z=6x-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6x-9$ (or $6x-z-9=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-6⟩=⟨1,0,6⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139534950 true] [17592186321952 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321953 13194139534950 true] [17592186321952 1089 "q-132579" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321952 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534950 true] [17592186321952 1100 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $x$-coordinate $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+xy$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321952 1113 #uuid "6336c497-86b6-5ccd-aae1-b53920cad5d4" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321952 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},2) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-1-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321954 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321955 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321956 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321957 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321958 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1041 "selection" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321958 13194139534950 true] [17592186321953 1110 #uuid "7dc65800-a9b6-58fe-99c5-342a293c07c0" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321954 1034 "2-\\pi " 13194139534950 true] [17592186321954 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186321954 1111 #uuid "9829bd35-dce6-543f-a40a-71578ce0965f" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321955 1034 "1+2\\pi " 13194139534950 true] [17592186321955 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186321955 1111 #uuid "7746ea2b-6e18-524f-b9cc-4542b80bad2f" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321956 1034 "\\pi -1" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186321956 1111 #uuid "779e5211-b5b7-5136-a9da-f5f7f40f9240" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321957 1034 "-\\pi " 13194139534950 true] [17592186321957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186321957 1111 #uuid "e05f54fa-df5e-5702-b9e2-4bf0c3be2f0c" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321958 1034 "-1-\\pi " 13194139534950 true] [17592186321958 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186321958 1111 #uuid "7c56a0dc-54e8-5f1a-903d-61ac8b42e52c" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321959 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321960 13194139534950 true] [17592186321959 1089 "q-132584" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321959 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534950 true] [17592186321959 1100 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Given that the point $P(1,-3,8)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}+y\\sqrt{x}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321959 1113 #uuid "81835159-51bf-5250-ba2c-6356bf0ceaa4" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321959 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-3,8)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-3) & =\\frac{{1}^{2}+1}{{1}^{2}(-3)^{2}}+(-3)\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}-3(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321960 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321961 13194139534950 true] [17592186321960 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321962 13194139534950 true] [17592186321960 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321963 13194139534950 true] [17592186321960 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321964 13194139534950 true] [17592186321960 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321965 13194139534950 true] [17592186321960 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [17592186321960 1041 "selection" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321960 1104 #e