⟨2,0,3⟩" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321998 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186321998 1111 #uuid "8b1543de-fbee-57f0-840f-09b15cf5f206" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321999 1034 "⟨2,0,-3⟩" 13194139534950 true] [17592186321999 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186321999 1111 #uuid "0f796832-1cc1-509b-afb5-012ac545dbd5" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322000 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322001 13194139534950 true] [17592186322000 1089 "q-36441" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322000 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534950 true] [17592186322000 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Given that the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=y+2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+\\frac{1}{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322000 1113 #uuid "34072283-dfb3-5acf-99b2-73755015acf1" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322000 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=0.5.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0.5,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}.$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(0.5,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =2+2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡((0.5)(2))+\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\  & =2+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0.5,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(0.5,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =4(y-2) \\\\ z & =4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0.5,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322002 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322003 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322004 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322005 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322006 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1041 "selection" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322004 13194139534950 true] [17592186322001 1110 #uuid "3954d628-4d5d-527a-90e5-ef106121e032" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322002 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322002 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322002 1111 #uuid "6da13587-3d3f-5470-a50e-7c5d0a75e2cd" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322003 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-12\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322003 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322003 1111 #uuid "10da0c28-49aa-51b3-81e1-2d18d2f02d2e" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322004 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=4y-4\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322004 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322004 1111 #uuid "c4c0ea80-ede6-524b-9322-8123deb65dfa" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322005 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+2\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322005 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322005 1111 #uuid "dc87c32f-2c60-5e05-a849-fd01a25f9b42" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322006 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}x=0.5 \\\\ z=-4y+12\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322006 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322006 1111 #uuid "519b4415-c57e-53b7-9b0e-92b9be6c80bc" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322007 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322008 13194139534950 true] [17592186322007 1089 "q-64968" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322007 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534950 true] [17592186322007 1100 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $0$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\sqrt{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+\\frac{\\pi }{4})$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322007 1113 #uuid "8cde8878-d1f7-5e10-aeab-7b3b6336ac1d" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322007 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=0$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =\\sqrt{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322009 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322010 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322011 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322012 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322013 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1041 "selection" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322010 13194139534950 true] [17592186322008 1110 #uuid "17da8771-82c2-5ba7-b963-1f0b8cb7839e" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322009 1034 "2" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322009 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322009 1111 #uuid "5eddbac7-927b-5213-85df-660169204d56" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322010 1034 "1" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322010 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322010 1111 #uuid "44dc2923-43e6-5ba8-ba14-60d522a61684" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322011 1034 "0" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322011 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322011 1111 #uuid "a4b89cf8-7d79-56d5-8db2-3531bec087a5" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322012 1034 "-1" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322012 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322012 1111 #uuid "c0d599d8-7400-5fcb-84b4-4a9d937b2604" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322013 1034 "-2" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322013 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322013 1111 #uuid "74f41e2a-620e-5649-9430-5390670de978" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322014 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322015 13194139534950 true] [17592186322014 1089 "q-64971" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322014 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534950 true] [17592186322014 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,1,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2(x+y)-2$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322014 1113 #uuid "2a3ebb85-3220-5af9-9101-50c80b1e42cb" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322014 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it." 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322016 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322017 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322018 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322019 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322020 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1041 "selection" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322016 13194139534950 true] [17592186322015 1110 #uuid "e1303ce5-14a0-59bc-a030-ea7ee71a4f4a" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322016 1034 "i+2\\,k" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322016 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322016 1111 #uuid "3e671d02-3be9-599f-8498-1f0470cf8072" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322017 1034 "-3i+\\,k" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322017 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322017 1111 #uuid "8cc02da0-c8a4-5fd0-aace-6fae8e8f22f6" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322018 1034 "i+\\,k" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322018 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322018 1111 #uuid "88a9c96f-283f-59b6-8c97-cb2d710d23f3" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322019 1034 "i-2\\,k" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322019 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322019 1111 #uuid "d72b8b3f-ed70-5049-83ed-e150e1e0ecfb" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322020 1034 "2i-2\\,k" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322020 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322020 1111 #uuid "ae641c86-c39b-5f04-9d92-346f62d73bd1" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322021 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322022 13194139534950 true] [17592186322021 1089 "q-71198" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322021 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534950 true] [17592186322021 1100 "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P$ with $y$-coordinate $1$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=3{x}^{2}y-6x{y}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322021 1113 #uuid "96ad954b-f367-5bd0-b4a5-cf923aa1850c" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322021 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point where $y=1$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(2,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(2,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,1) & =3(2)^{2}(1)-6(2)(1)^{2} \\\\  & =12-12 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322023 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322024 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322025 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322026 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322027 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1041 "selection" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322026 13194139534950 true] [17592186322022 1110 #uuid "53f6c136-16f6-5d6c-9327-383bceb91c49" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322023 1034 "2" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322023 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322023 1111 #uuid "4c30d2a9-e439-5868-bf6a-f0943eccad48" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322024 1034 "1" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322024 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322024 1111 #uuid "f64cf730-beaf-5858-a45c-16cd6bc80fb9" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322025 1034 "-3" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322025 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322025 1111 #uuid "7361735c-97e0-5615-b0c6-3793c9609d0a" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322026 1034 "0" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322026 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322026 1111 #uuid "03c59a88-c6f7-5794-88ea-8e1dca5385fd" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322027 1034 "-1" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322027 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322027 1111 #uuid "f4b3a63c-6270-52af-92fe-374dadfaa588" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322028 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322029 13194139534950 true] [17592186322028 1089 "q-71218" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322028 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534950 true] [17592186322028 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,2)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322028 1113 #uuid "76f63137-b52c-5f1c-a959-b140c362941f" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322028 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(0,1,2)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)} & =(0)^{2}+(1)^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(0,1)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(0,1)}(x-0) \\\\ z-2 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,2)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322030 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322031 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322032 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322033 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322034 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1041 "selection" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322031 13194139534950 true] [17592186322029 1110 #uuid "b5255667-5bb3-563c-b8ce-809e544e6d69" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322030 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-2\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322030 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322030 1111 #uuid "6626448e-5b4f-5f98-8a20-7682838c8c0e" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322031 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+2\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322031 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322031 1111 #uuid "f6f50bb7-8a2a-5602-8722-ed0ffb4f477f" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322032 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x-2\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322032 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322032 1111 #uuid "5aa8f47b-37f9-57d6-b89b-788e69c98aaa" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322033 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=-x+2\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322033 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322033 1111 #uuid "11905ccd-6edb-5a46-b398-0390aea63059" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322034 1034 "{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2\\end{aligned}" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322034 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322034 1111 #uuid "1687ea78-571c-5420-b775-22dadb21cbac" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322035 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322036 13194139534950 true] [17592186322035 1089 "q-71220" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322035 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534950 true] [17592186322035 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-6,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{x(y+x)}}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322035 1113 #uuid "daf286ba-b2e7-5950-80df-28aebe5b694f" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322035 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-6).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-6) & =\\frac{-6-(-2)}{\\sqrt{(-2)(-6+(-2))}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,-6) & ={f}_{y}(-2,-6)(y-(-6)) \\\\ z-1 & =-1(y+6) \\\\ z-1 & =-y-6 \\\\ z & =-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,-6,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=-y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-5$ (or $y+z+5=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,-1,1⟩.$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322037 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322038 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322039 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322040 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322041 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1041 "selection" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322038 13194139534950 true] [17592186322036 1110 #uuid "cf8da1d4-0854-5f96-a089-243dfb234ab5" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322037 1034 "⟨0,-6,1⟩" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322037 1111 #uuid "cf0840cc-8f40-554d-aa27-2ab328a01c38" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322038 1034 "⟨0,-1,1⟩" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322038 1111 #uuid "991daaff-2d46-56b2-9cb6-3ae09a927011" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322039 1034 "⟨0,-2,1⟩" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322039 1111 #uuid "ea12e558-b041-5760-8e95-5223f775f3a9" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322040 1034 "⟨0,1,1⟩" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322040 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322040 1111 #uuid "81541cd4-92b4-580d-94a8-5fc9d7efa355" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322041 1034 "⟨0,6,1⟩" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322041 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534950 true] [17592186322041 1111 #uuid "029a2e0e-0f4e-51ca-a7f3-34f2594d5f9f" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322042 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322043 13194139534950 true] [17592186322042 1089 "q-71221" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322042 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534950 true] [17592186322042 1100 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,1,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=3{e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|xy|$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322042 1113 #uuid "23da9fa3-3fe5-5812-be11-6d830c793b0c" 13194139534950 true] [17592186322042 1145 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-1,1) & =3{e}^{-1+1}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(-1)\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,1) & ={f}_{x}(-1,1)(x-(-1)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =3(x+1) \\\\ z+1 & =3x+3 \\\\ z & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,1,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3x+2$ (or $3x-z+2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-3\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-3⟩=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it." 13194139534950 true] [17592186322043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322044 13194139534950 true] [17592186322043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322045 13194139534950 true] [17592186322043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322046 13194139534950 true] [17592186322043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322047 13194139534950 true] [17592186322043 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322048 13194139534950 true] [17592186322043 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534950 true] [175921